# The domain comes from SPLOUCH_DOMAIN in cloud/.env — do not write it in here.
#
# This file is tracked by git. A deploy that resets the working tree (the Update
# button's master and branch modes) reverts anything edited here, so a literal domain
# would silently go back to the placeholder and Caddy would serve the wrong site — and
# fail to renew the certificate — from its next restart. `.env` is untracked, so the
# domain survives every update.
#
# Caddy obtains and renews the Let's Encrypt certificate automatically.
#
# Two upstreams on one domain (docs/architecture/scaling.md, stage 2). A meet's
# live paths go to the relay worker, which holds it in memory: the Pi's and the
# attendees' sockets, the meet pages, and the per-meet manifest and icon. Everything
# else — the picker, /admin, /privacy, /add, the locale tables — is the control
# plane's. When meets live on other nodes, the picker hands each one's host to the
# client and this split stops being the only way in.
#
# The control plane's route is the catch-all, `/.well-known/` included. That is
# load-bearing for the QR hand-off (docs/app.md `P-16`) — the control plane serves
# `/.well-known/assetlinks.json` and `/.well-known/apple-app-site-association`, and
# Android fetches the first at install time following **no redirects**. A `handle`
# or `file_server` block added above the catch-all that matched them would swallow
# them and the only symptom would be `pm get-app-links app.splouch.android`
# reporting `1024` on a phone, weeks later. Caddy's own ACME handler is scoped to
# `/.well-known/acme-challenge/*` and does not overlap.
#
# `/internal/*` is the workers' API. Here the worker reaches the control plane over
# the compose network, so the public route refuses it outright; the NODE_SECRET
# every call carries is the gate once a worker on another server has to come in
# over HTTPS.
{$SPLOUCH_DOMAIN} {
    @worker path /ws/* /mobile /mobile/* /meet/* /manifest/* /icon/*
    handle @worker {
        reverse_proxy app:5000
    }
    handle /internal/* {
        respond 404
    }
    handle {
        reverse_proxy control:8000
    }
}
